  },
  "note": "Plant: Brown, Welch & Cary (1987). Human: no EFSA dietary reference value."
 },
 "history": {
  "discovered": 1751,
  "discoverers": [
   "Axel Fredrik Cronstedt"
  ],
  "place": "Stockholm, Sweden",
  "nameOrigin": "Shortened from the German Kupfernickel, a miners' name (roughly 'copper demon') for the ore niccolite, which looked like copper ore but yielded no copper.",
  "source": "pubchem"
 },
 "prose": {
  "text": "Nickel is a silvery, corrosion-resistant metal used mainly in stainless steel, as well as in coins and rechargeable batteries; it also forms, with iron, the metal of most iron meteorites. Plants need a trace of it for urease, the enzyme that breaks down urea. In the nucleus, nickel holds a record: nickel-62 has the highest binding energy per nucleon of any nucleus, about 8.79 MeV, slightly more than iron-56. The free atom's ground configuration is [Ar] 3d⁸ 4s² (term ³F₄), but a level from the rival arrangement 3d⁹ 4s¹ lies only 0.025 eV above it, so the two are almost tied.",
  "sources": [
   "iaea_livechart",
   "nist_levels",
   "nist_gsie"
  ]
 }
}
